WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:08.100
Kubernetes workload security includes supply chain, admission policy, observability, and release governance.

2
00:00:08.200 --> 00:00:20.760
The workload that reaches the cluster is the result of source code, base images, NuGet packages, build systems, registries, manifest generation, approvals, and deployment automation.

3
00:00:21.040 --> 00:00:34.560
Trusted registries, least-privilege registry access, image scanning, SBOMs, signed images or provenance metadata where available, and clear promotion workflows all support production confidence.

4
00:00:34.660 --> 00:00:39.380
Teams should know what image is running, where it came from, how it was approved, and how it can be rolled back.

5
00:00:39.660 --> 00:00:51.020
Admission policies can enforce requirements such as non-root runtime, trusted registries, required resource limits, restricted capabilities, approved labels, and disallowed host access.

6
00:00:51.120 --> 00:00:58.880
Policy should be visible to developers early enough that secure requirements are designed into workloads rather than discovered only at release time.

7
00:00:59.160 --> 00:01:12.120
Logs should support troubleshooting without exposing secrets, tokens, authorization headers, cookies, connection strings, raw request bodies, mounted secret contents, or sensitive business data.

8
00:01:12.220 --> 00:01:22.940
Production diagnostics, exec access, port-forwarding, debug containers, crash dumps, and metrics endpoints should be access-controlled and environment-scoped.

9
00:01:23.220 --> 00:01:35.860
Production review should cover image, manifest, namespace, service account, RBAC, secrets, network exposure, probes, resource settings, logs, rollback, and incident response.

10
00:01:35.960 --> 00:01:42.280
The best Kubernetes controls are practical enough to be used consistently and observable enough to support safe operations.
